{"title":"Say What You Mean","markdown":{"headingText":"Say What You Mean","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-say-what-you-mean","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n> The AI knows what you said. It does not know what you meant.\n\n**Lab companion:** [Lab 3, Say What You Mean](https://michael-borck.github.io/programming-labs/lab-03-say-what-you-mean/) — the standalone taster: one agent, one empty folder, one spec.\n\n## The Concept First\n\nWhen a human contractor takes a brief, they bring a lifetime of unstated context. You say \"build a booking page\" and they silently supply a thousand decisions — what a booking *is*, what sensible means, what the culture expects a form to look like. The contractor's filler knowledge hides the gaps in your brief.\n\nThe agent has the same filler knowledge about the *world*, and none about *your world*. So the gaps in your brief do not get filled with sense. They get filled with the statistical average of the entire internet — which is fluent, plausible, and almost never your business.\n\nA **specification** is the brief with the gaps closed. Not a genre exercise: a working document whose test is brutally simple. Hand it, alone, to a fresh agent that has never seen your conversation. If what comes back is a product you would accept, you have authored a program in English. The labs call this the **Replication Test**, and it is the closest thing this book has to a definition of done for thinking.\n\n## Understanding Through the Recipe\n\nA recipe that says \"season to taste\" assumes a cook. A recipe that says \"two teaspoons of salt\" assumes nothing. Both produce food; only one survives being handed to a stranger in a strange kitchen.\n\nSpecifications are the second kind. The test is not \"did the agent understand me?\" — it is \"would a stranger, with only this document, produce the same thing?\" If the answer depends on the conversation you had before the document, the document is not the program. The conversation was.\n\n## Discovering Specification with Your AI Partner\n\n### Exploration 1: Feel the Gap\n\nAsk for something real, deliberately under-specified:\n\n```text\nBuild me a tip calculator\n```\n\nThen interrogate the result: What currency? Round up or exact? Split bills? What happens with a zero bill? Every answer the agent invented was a gap you owned and did not close. List the gaps. That list is your specification, discovered backwards.\n\n### Exploration 2: Close the Gaps in Order\n\nNow write the specification — but write it as a document the agent must read *before* doing anything:\n\n```text\nRead this specification completely before writing any code.\n\nSPEC — Tip Calculator v1\n\nPurpose: split a restaurant bill N ways with tip.\n\nInputs:\n- bill total (number, currency of the bill, must be > 0)\n- number of people (whole number, 1 to 20)\n- tip percentage (whole number, 0 to 50, default 20)\n\nBehaviour:\n- Each person pays an equal share of (bill + tip).\n- Shares are rounded to the nearest cent; rounding residue goes to\n  the first person.\n- Invalid inputs stop processing with a clear message. No defaults\n  for invalid bills or party sizes.\n\nOut of scope for v1: currencies other than the bill's own, tax\ncalculation, saving history.\n\nDone means: all of the above demonstrated with the five examples\nbelow, plus any case in my hostile list.\n```\n\n### Exploration 3: The Replication Test\n\nOpen a completely fresh conversation — no memory of the last one. Paste only the specification, followed by:\n\n```text\nBuild exactly this. If anything in the spec is ambiguous or\ncontradictory, stop and ask.\n```\n\nCompare the two builds. Differences between them are the spec's remaining gaps — the places where you were still relying on a shared conversation that a stranger would not have. Close them, and run the test again. When a fresh agent converges on something you would accept, the spec passes.\n\n::: {.callout-important title=\"The Replication Test is the book's definition of authorship\"}\nYou did not type the code. You do not need to. You wrote the document that reproduces the product from nothing — and that document, not the code, is what you authored. This is what \"programming at the natural-language layer\" means, precisely.\n:::\n\n## From Concept to Code\n\nThe specification lives in the repository, named and versioned like any other file:\n\n```bash\ngit add SPEC.md\ngit commit -m \"Add tip calculator spec v1: closes splitting ambiguities\"\n```\n\nFrom now on, every project in this book carries a `SPEC.md`. When the agent's work is evaluated — by you, by a colleague, by the audit in Chapter 15 — the question is never \"is this code nice?\" It is \"does this meet the spec, and would the spec reproduce it?\"\n\n::: {.callout-tip title=\"The hostile read\"}\nBefore running the Replication Test, read your own spec looking for the word \"it\" and any word you would have to gesture at to explain (\"nice\", \"clean\", \"user-friendly\", \"handle properly\"). Every one is a gap. Replace it with the example that would settle a disagreement.\n:::\n\n## Business Connection\n\nThe Replication Test is not a programmer's trick — it is how organisations stop losing knowledge. Any document that only makes sense next to the person who wrote it is a liability with a resignation date. Specifications that pass a stranger test survive staff turnover, vendor changes, and the next model upgrade, because the meaning finally lives in the artefact, not the memory.\n\nThis is also the quiet answer to \"is prompting a real skill?\" Prompting is improvisation. Specification is authorship. The first fades with the chat history; the second is an asset on your books.\n\n## Practice Exercises\n\n**Exercise 4.1 (Level 1).** Run the tip-calculator Replication Test exactly as written. Record where the two builds differ.\n\n**Exercise 4.2 (Level 2).** Write the spec for \"a contact form\" — then find five gaps by reading it as a stranger. Close them.\n\n**Exercise 4.3 (Level 3).** Take a real work email in which you asked someone to do something. Convert it to a spec that would pass the stranger test. Count the gaps you closed.\n\n**Exercise 4.4 (Level 4).** Write a spec where *deliberately* leaving one decision open is the right call — and specify how the open decision must be reported back.\n\n**Exercise 4.5 (Level 5).** Write the spec for your Chapter 3 project's next version (v2), run the Replication Test with a fresh agent, and file both builds. The diff between them is your spec's fingerprint.\n\n## Chapter Summary\n\n- The agent fills your brief's gaps with the statistical average of the internet\n- A specification closes the gaps; the Replication Test proves it\n- You authored a program in English when a stranger's build matches your intent\n- The spec lives in the repository, versioned — it outlives the chat\n\n## Reflection\n\nBefore moving on, ensure you:\n\n- [ ] Ran a Replication Test with a genuinely fresh conversation\n- [ ] Found and closed at least three gaps by comparing builds\n- [ ] Committed a SPEC.md that passes the stranger test\n- [ ] Can explain the difference between prompting and specifying\n\n## Your Learning Journal\n\n1. What did the fresh agent invent that the briefed agent never would?\n2. Which gap was hardest to close — and is that gap actually an undecided decision?\n3. What in your work life would survive a \"stranger test\" today?\n\n## Next Steps\n\nThe spec says what done means. The next chapter makes done enforceable: a checklist the department cannot charm its way past — because it runs.\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":false,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb